only because there are no grades yet, so the one-grade rule had nothing to check.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **`gnd` bug:** my first version of the credit total crashed `gnd check` with an internal error (exit 70). I changed how it's derived to work around it. The catch is that a population now has to state each student's credit total for the 18-credit rule to run. If it leaves the total out, the check reports \"indeterminate\" rather than passing, so nothing slips through silently. This is worth reporting upstream.\n- **Downstream tools:** `gnd synth` and `gnd codegen` don't accept a model with a fixed catalogue. If you need them, the catalogue can be turned into ordinary facts instead.\n- **My own inferences are advisory only.** These are: prerequisites can't form a loop, every course has one title and one credit value, and every graded course counts as passed. None of them can fail a check until you confirm them.\n\n**Questions for the registrar:**\n1. If an operation changes something it didn't declare, should that be a warning or a hard failure? It has to be stated either way, so I set warning for every operation.\n2. Recording a grade removes the enrollment, so \"enrolled\" means \"currently registered\". I chose this so that \"no enrolling in a passed course\" doesn't clash with keeping grades forever. Is that right?\n3. Can a grade be a fail? As written, every recorded grade marks the course as passed.\n4. Are students identified by name, or do they have a student number?\n5. Terms are just \"spring\" and \"fall\", with no year. Should a term carry a year?", "ttft_ms": 2146, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 429083, "uuid": "17d63e4a-a426-45a9-a81c-6ee00839527c", "ttft_stream_ms": 1593, "time_to_request_ms": 33, "first_content_frame_ms": 1593, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
